# Container/namespace only. Batch 3 of the Guerrero event set: the villages, the land, the
# column's inner life, prisoners, and the outside world. All banda="sierra", f_phase="marcha".

# =====================================================================
# LA TIERRA Y LOS PUEBLOS
# =====================================================================
@ev_cacique
title: El cacique de la Costa Grande
new-page: true
face-image: img/guardias_blancas.jpg
subtitle: The man who owns the water rights, the store, and the judge
tags: event
view-if: banda = "sierra" and f_phase = "marcha" and time >= f_ev_last + 2
priority: 4
frequency: 55
max-visits: 3
on-arrival: f_ev_last = time

= El cacique de la Costa Grande

Every valley in Guerrero has one: the man who owns the coffee-buying scale that always reads light, the only truck, the store that sells on credit at rates that make a harvest disappear, and a cousin in the municipal presidency. He is not the army and he is not the Party, but he is how both of them actually touch these villages. The assembly asks you, carefully, whether the guerrilla intends to do anything about him — and the way you answer will define what kind of power you are here.

- @cac_expropiar: Expropriate the store and the scale — hand the ledgers to the assembly and let the debts burn. *(+apoyo, +funds; he has friends with guns)*
- @cac_negociar: Make him a proposition: he stays, he stops stealing, he pays a tax to the movement. *(+funds, quiet; the villages notice you spared him)*
- @cac_ignorar: Leave him. You are fighting a state, not a shopkeeper, and every enemy you make is a rifle pointed at your back.

@cac_expropiar
on-arrival: {! var _h=Math.max(0,(Q.apoyo_techo||55)-(Q.apoyo_popular||0)); Q.apoyo_popular+=Math.min(6,_h*0.35); Q.funds=(Q.funds||0)+8; Q.f_comida+=8; Q.sup_peasants+=3; Q.f_cerco+=8; Q.legitimidad_regimen-=1; !}

You burn the debt ledgers in the plaza, which is the oldest revolutionary theatre there is and still the most effective — half the valley owed him money their grandparents borrowed. The store's stock goes to the assembly to distribute. He leaves for Acapulco that night and comes back three weeks later with the guardias blancas and a lieutenant who owes him favours, and the war in this valley gets sharper. Worth it. The people will remember the fire.

- @root: Continuar.

@cac_negociar
on-arrival: {! Q.funds=(Q.funds||0)+14; Q.f_comida+=6; Q.apoyo_popular-=2; Q.inteligencia+=2; !}

You sit with him in his own back room and explain the arithmetic: the movement can burn his store tonight, or he can keep it and pay for the privilege, and also stop cheating the scale. He is a practical man; practical men survive revolutions. The money is good and the intelligence better — he hears everything. But the campesinos watched the guerrilla walk into the cacique's house and walk out again with an understanding, and some of them file that away.

- @root: Continuar.

@cac_ignorar
on-arrival: {! Q.f_cerco-=3; if(Q.f_cerco<0){Q.f_cerco=0;} Q.apoyo_popular-=3; !}

You let him be. Strategically it is defensible — he is a small man and the column has an army to fight — and it is also the moment a few campesinos privately conclude that this guerrilla is not, in the end, about *them*. The state is far away and abstract. The cacique is the boot they actually feel.

- @root: Continuar.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
@ev_tierras
title: El reparto
new-page: true
face-image: img/toma_tierras.jpg
subtitle: The land, taken — in the one place where the guerrilla is the government
tags: event
view-if: banda = "sierra" and f_phase = "marcha" and apoyo_popular >= 45 and time >= f_ev_last + 3
priority: 5
frequency: 50
max-visits: 3
on-arrival: f_ev_last = time

= El reparto

In the valley where you are strongest, the campesinos come with a proposal that is more revolutionary than any operation you have run: they want to take the land. Not petition for it, not file with the agrarian department that has been losing their papers since 1940 — *take* it, the terrenos the finca has held since before the Revolution, and work them, and let the guerrilla be the thing that makes it stick.

- @tie_repartir: Do it. Survey it with them, divide it, and stand behind the result with rifles. *(big +apoyo; the finqueros scream; the army comes)*
- @tie_esperar: Not yet — a reparto you cannot defend is a massacre with paperwork. Promise it for when the zone is secure. *(no gain; the moment passes)*

@tie_repartir
on-arrival: {! var _h=Math.max(0,(Q.apoyo_techo||55)-(Q.apoyo_popular||0)); Q.apoyo_popular+=Math.min(10,_h*0.5); Q.sup_peasants+=6; Q.zona_gobernada=1; Q.f_comida+=10; Q.legitimidad_regimen-=3; Q.f_cerco+=12; Q.leyenda=(Q.leyenda||0)+2; !}

It takes four days with a rope and an argument at every boundary, and at the end of it families who have worked that ground for three generations own it in the only sense the sierra recognizes: everyone agrees they do, and there are rifles that say so. This is the thing the state promised for fifty years and delivered as a filing cabinet. The finqueros are in Chilpancingo by the weekend demanding the army, and the army will come. Let it. The reparto is done, and undoing it would mean evicting four hundred armed families.

- @root: Continuar.

@tie_esperar
on-arrival: {! Q.apoyo_popular-=3; Q.f_cerco-=4; if(Q.f_cerco<0){Q.f_cerco=0;} !}

You tell them the truth, which is that the army would come and burn the harvest and shoot the ringleaders and the guerrilla could not stop it. They nod, because they know it too. They also hear the thing under the sentence: *not yet* is what the government has been saying since their grandfathers. You have been careful and correct, and slightly diminished.

- @root: Continuar.

